course: biology
lesson: 343
title: Where meat and fish come from, told honestly
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A sausage at a barbecue is mostly minced pork, and pork is meat from a pig. Meat is the muscle of an animal that was alive and was killed for food. It is not a nice story, but it is a true one, and it is better to know.
explain: Most farm animals in Britain are taken to an abattoir, also called a slaughterhouse, and the law says they must usually be made unconscious first so they do not feel it. Some people feel fine about eating meat, some feel unsure and some choose not to eat it at all, and all of these are real choices.
explain: Carve a roast chicken and you can count two legs, two wings and two breasts, each a different muscle. The legs are dark because the chicken walked all day, and the breast is pale because it hardly flew.
explain: Fish come from the wild sea or from fish farms, like the round floating net pens in Scottish lochs. Catch too many and there are not enough left to have babies, which is what happened to the cod off Canada.
fact: Meat is the muscle of an animal that was killed for food; in Britain the law says animals at an abattoir must usually be made unconscious first so they do not feel it.
fact: After 1066 English-speaking farmers kept the animals but French-speaking Norman lords ate the meat, so we say cow, pig and sheep but beef, pork and mutton; chicken and fish kept one name.
fact: Dark meat has more myoglobin, a red protein that stores oxygen in muscles that work for a long time, so a chicken's legs are dark and its breast is pale, while ducks and wild pigeons that fly a lot have dark breast meat.
fact: A chicken has two legs, two wings and two breasts; the wishbone is its two collarbones joined together.
fact: Fish fingers are usually white fish such as cod, pollock or haddock, cut into blocks and breadcrumbed, and were first sold in Britain in 1955.
fact: Most salmon eaten in Britain comes from fish farms with big net pens in the sea; crowded pens can spread sea lice and disease to wild fish.
fact: Wild salmon are pink because they eat tiny shrimp with a pink colour in them, and farmed salmon get the same colour added to their food.
fact: Around Newfoundland so many cod were caught that the fishing was closed in 1992, thousands of people lost their jobs, and decades later the cod have still not fully come back.
fact: A blue fish label on packets shows fish from a fishery checked as well managed.
fact: Fish have many more small bones than mammals, such as the thin pin bones in salmon, while sharks have no bones at all, only bendy cartilage.
fact: Vegetarians eat no meat or fish, and vegans eat no food from animals at all; many Hindus do not eat beef, and Muslims and Jews do not eat pork.
fact: Beans, lentils, nuts, tofu and eggs give plenty of protein to people who do not eat meat.
fact: Raw meat, especially chicken, can carry germs such as Salmonella and Campylobacter; cooking all the way through kills them, and raw chicken should not be washed under the tap because splashes spread germs.
exercise: What is meat? | The muscle of an animal that was killed for food | A plant grown in big fields that is made to taste just like an animal | A food made in factories from flour | The fat an animal sheds each year
exercise: What must usually happen to an animal at an abattoir in Britain before it is killed? | It must be made unconscious so it does not feel it | It must be washed all over in warm water and then dried with towels | It must be fed one last big meal | Nothing, there are no rules at all
exercise: What animal does pork come from? | A pig | A dairy cow | A sheep | A deer
exercise: Why do we say «beef» but «cow»? | After 1066 the Norman lords who ate the meat spoke French | Farmers wanted a secret word for the meat so that children would not be upset | Beef is the old word for a baby cow | Cows were renamed by the Romans
exercise: Why is a chicken's leg meat darker than its breast? | Leg muscles work all day and hold more myoglobin | The legs get much more sunshine while the chicken walks about | The breast is bleached white when cooked | Leg meat is from an older chicken
exercise: What is a chicken's wishbone? | Its two collarbones joined together | The little bone at the end of its tail | A bone from its wing | A bone from the top of its leg
exercise: Which fish are fish fingers usually made from? | White fish such as cod, pollock or haddock | Salmon and tuna, cut up and painted white | Baby sharks | Goldfish bred on special farms for fish fingers
exercise: Where does most salmon eaten in Britain come from? | Fish farms with net pens in the sea | Rivers running through the middle of London | The deep sea near the North Pole | Fish tanks in shops
exercise: Why are wild salmon pink? | They eat tiny shrimp with a pink colour in them | They are pink from birth whatever they eat | Cold water turns them pink | They are dyed by fishermen
exercise: What is one problem with crowded salmon farms? | Sea lice and disease can spread to wild fish | The salmon slowly turn into cod when there are too many of them | The pens make the sea freeze | The fish grow too big to swim
exercise: Why was cod fishing off Newfoundland closed in 1992? | So many cod had been caught that the stock collapsed | The sea there froze solid for the whole of that year | The cod all swam away to Africa | People stopped liking the taste of cod
exercise: What does a blue fish label on a packet show? | The fish came from a fishery checked as well managed | The fish is a special kind of fish that has blue skin and blue meat | The fish was frozen for a year | The fish was caught by a child
exercise: What are sharks' skeletons made of? | Bendy cartilage, not bone | Thick, heavy bone like ours | Hard shell, just like a crab's | Nothing, they have no skeleton
exercise: What does a vegan not eat? | Any food from animals, even milk, eggs and honey | Only red meat such as beef and lamb, but chicken is fine | Only fish and other things from the sea | Only vegetables
exercise: Which food gives protein to someone who eats no meat? | Lentils | Lettuce leaves | Apple juice | White sugar
exercise: Why should you not wash raw chicken under the tap? | Splashes spread germs around the sink | It makes the chicken meat tough and chewy | Water turns raw chicken meat a grey colour | It washes away all the good vitamins inside
test: «sausages are made of, like, sausage plants?» What is the honest answer? | They are mostly minced pork, meat from pigs | Yeah, they grow in bunches on bushes in Germany | They are made from bread and herbs only | They come from a sea creature
test: In Finding Nemo, Bruce the shark promises «fish are friends, not food». Which is true of a real shark's body? | It has no bones, only bendy cartilage | It has more small bones than any other fish | It is a mammal, like a whale or dolphin | It has bones made of hard shell like a crab
test: Count them: Dad carves 2 roast chickens. How many legs, wings and wishbones are there? | 4 legs, 4 wings and 2 wishbones | 2 legs, 2 wings and 2 wishbones | 4 legs, 4 wings and 4 wishbones | 8 legs, 4 wings and 2 wishbones
test: Would a goose that flies thousands of kilometres have dark or pale breast meat? | Dark, because its flying muscles work for a long time | Pale, like a chicken's | Bright green | Clear like jelly
test: «just rinse the chicken mate, gets the germs off» What is the best answer? | No, rinsing splashes germs about; cooking all the way through kills them | Yes, water from the tap kills all the germs on raw meat straight away | Yes, but use cold water only | No, you should rinse it in the bath
test: What would happen if a farmed salmon got no pink colour in its food? | Its meat would be pale grey | Its meat would be bright red | It would slowly turn into a cod | Nothing, salmon make pink colour from water
test: «my mate Aisha won't eat the ham sandwich, she's just fussy» What is the most likely reason? | Muslims do not eat pork, and ham is pork | Ham is really made from fish, and she does not like fish | She is allergic to bread | Ham is only for grown-ups
test: A fishing boat takes almost all the adult fish from a bay. What will happen in a few years? | There will be few fish, because not enough adults were left to have babies | There will be more fish than ever, because the young ones have the whole bay to themselves | The fish will grow back in a week | Nothing changes, the sea has endless fish
test: «why are the fish fingers boneless, did they breed fish without bones?» What is the true answer? | The white fish is cut into blocks with the bones taken out | Yes, fish fingers come from a special fish that has no bones | The bones melt away in the cold of the freezer | They are made from sharks only
test: «eat your fish slowly» says Gran. Why does she have a point? | Fish have many small bones that are easy to swallow by mistake | Fish go cold faster than meat | Fish is only good for you if eaten slowly | Fast eating turns fish into meat
test: In a cartoon a pig happily walks into a sausage machine and comes out as links. What is wrong? | Animals are killed first, and the law says they must usually be made unconscious first | Nothing is wrong, that is just how sausages are made at the factory | Sausages come from chickens only | Pigs walk out alive at the other end
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the English language employs distinct terms for certain animals and for their meat. | Following 1066, the meat was consumed by Norman lords who spoke French whereas the animals were kept by English speakers | The terms were introduced by Roman soldiers | The distinction was required by medieval law | The terms derive from the noises produced by the animals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises dark meat? | It contains more myoglobin, a protein storing oxygen in muscles used for long periods | It is derived exclusively from aged animals that have been kept for many years before slaughter | It is darkened by the process of cooking alone | It contains no protein whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the consequence of the excessive capture of cod in the vicinity of Newfoundland. | The stock collapsed, fishing was closed in 1992, and recovery has remained incomplete for decades | The cod relocated permanently to warmer waters | The population doubled in the subsequent year | No measurable consequence was observed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the safe handling of raw poultry. | It should be cooked thoroughly and must not be rinsed under the tap, since splashing disperses germs | It should be rinsed thoroughly under the hot tap before cooking so that all germs are removed from it | It may be consumed raw provided it is fresh | It should be stored on the uppermost shelf of the refrigerator
